Sports injuries
Young Adult & Adolecence
Localized swelling, Pain on movement, Inability to bear weight

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-040
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe localized joint pain and massive soft tissue swelling rendering the patient entirely unable to bear weight following a high-impact sporting injury.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Sibling
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an acute, high-energy inversion injury to the ankle accompanied by an audible 'pop', immediate massive swelling, and a complete inability to stand. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was critically required to exclude an unstable bi-malleolar fracture, assess for acute neurovascular compromise, and provide immediate joint stabilization and potent analgesia prior to the next regular GP session.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I rolled my ankle coming down from a rebound, heard a loud pop, and now the pain is blinding and I can't put any weight on it at all."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a sudden onset of severe, localized joint pain in the right ankle, sustained 2 hours ago during a basketball game. The patient vividly describes an audible pop and snapping sensation at the moment of injury. Since the event, there has been rapid, massive localized soft tissue swelling and oedema over the lateral aspect of the ankle. The pain is severe, constant, and sharply exacerbated by any movement. The patient reports a complete inability to fully bear weight, resulting in a profoundly antalgic gait when attempting to hop with assistance.
Functional Status: Bedbound; absolutely unable to weight-bear on the right lower extremity.
Social/Safety Concerns: High acute anxiety regarding a potential fracture requiring surgical intervention; inability to ambulate independently.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/82 | HR: 98 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Patient is visibly distressed, grimacing in pain, and aggressively guarding the right ankle.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal examination reveals massive, tense localized swelling and early ecchymosis tracking distally from the lateral malleolus towards the base of the 5th metatarsal. There is exquisite, localized point tenderness over the anterior talofibular ligament (ATFL) and calcaneofibular ligament (CFL). Crucially, palpation of the posterior edge of the lateral and medial malleoli, the navicular, and the base of the 5th metatarsal (Ottawa Ankle Rules) reveals severe bony tenderness at the lateral malleolus. Active and passive range of motion is severely restricted by pain. Neurovascular status is intact (capillary refill <2 seconds, normal pedal pulses, normal sensation). The presentation perfectly aligns with the diagnostic tetrad of localized swelling, pain on movement, and inability to bear weight.
Environmental Observations: Ice was applied sporadically, but the leg was completely dependent (hanging down), significantly worsening the acute oedema.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient took Paracetamol 1000mg with zero efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Severe Ankle Sprain (Grade III) with Suspected Lateral Malleolus Fracture - Status: Acute Injury
Differentials:

Achilles Tendon Rupture - Ruled out/unlikely due to the pain and swelling localizing firmly to the lateral ankle ligaments rather than the posterior heel, and an intact plantar flexion mechanism on the Thompson test.

Syndesmotic (High) Ankle Sprain - Ruled out/unlikely due to the negative squeeze test (no pain produced above the ankle joint when compressing the tibia and fibula at the mid-calf).

Compartment Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of normal, bounding distal pulses, intact sensation, and soft, compressible compartments in the calf musculature.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (Medical Risk: Potential fracture requiring casting)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient sustained a high-energy inversion injury resulting in immediate inability to bear weight, massive swelling, and an audible pop. The application of the Ottawa Ankle Rules during the physical examination revealed specific bony tenderness over the lateral malleolus, indicating a high probability of an acute fracture. Delaying assessment until the next day would have subjected the patient to unmanageable agonizing pain, worsened the massive oedema, and risked displacement of a potential fracture. The immediate after-hours home visit allowed for safe exclusion of vascular compromise, immediate rigid splinting of the joint, and provision of strong analgesia, facilitating a safe, controlled transfer for urgent radiographic imaging.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Ketorolac 30mg IM stat to rapidly decrease acute pain and localized inflammation. Applied a rigid posterior backslab splint and compression bandage to entirely immobilize the ankle joint. Generated an urgent referral for a plain radiograph of the right ankle and foot to formally diagnose or exclude a fracture, and to evaluate for focal ligamentous disruption.
Positive Behavioral Change: To rapidly halt the massive soft tissue swelling, strictly elevate the injured joint on two pillows so it rests above the level of the heart continuously for the next 48 hours, directly utilizing gravity to drain the oedema and reduce the throbbing pain. To protect the compromised joint from further catastrophic failure, utilize the provided crutches for all ambulation, explicitly rewarding the use of the mobility aid with the total prevention of agonizing weight-bearing pain.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Fitted with axillary crutches and instructed on safe non-weight-bearing mobilization.
Follow-up: Urgent review at the local urgent care centre or ED within 12 hours for the plain X-ray and definitive orthopedic management (e.g., CAM boot or casting).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department via ambulance is required if the toes suddenly become cold, pale, or blue, if severe numbness develops in the foot, or if the calf muscle becomes tight, rigid, and agonizingly painful.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The physical examination strongly suggests a severe ligament tear and a possible bone fracture; an X-ray is absolutely required to confirm the extent of the damage.
Clinical Course: The injected painkiller and the rigid splint will significantly reduce the sharp pain, but the joint will remain swollen and bruised for several weeks regardless of whether a fracture is present.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Go to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if your toes turn blue or grey, if you lose feeling in your foot, or if your calf muscle suddenly becomes incredibly hard and painful.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The violent twist of your ankle has severely torn the ligaments that hold the joint together, and may have chipped or broken the bone, which is why the swelling is so massive. The rigid splint and crutches are applied to completely lock the joint in place, preventing any sharp bone edges from causing further damage while you get an X-ray.
Actionable Steps: Do not put a single ounce of weight on that foot under any circumstances. Keep the ankle elevated above your chest while lying down to stop blood from pooling and causing more throbbing pain.

